First, let's find an expression for $G'(x)$ in terms of $f'$ and $g'$:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}G'(x)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}G(x)&&\gray{\text{Equivalent notation}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}[-4f(x)+3g(x)-2]&&\gray{\text{Substitute the expression for }G(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}[-4f(x)]+\dfrac{d}{dx}[3g(x)]-\dfrac{d}{dx}(2)&&\gray{\text{Sum and difference rules}}
\\\\
&=-4f'(x)+3g'(x)-0&&\gray{\text{Constant and constant multiple rules}}
\end{align}$

So we found that $G'(x)=-4f'(x)+3g'(x)$.
